WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.320
Dueling volcanos and rival&nbsp;
expeditions to Mars. Would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.560
you believe that these very similar&nbsp;
movies were released back to back?

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:13.620
All the way back in 1883, Italian author&nbsp;
Carlo Collodi shared his best known work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:18.600
"The Adventures of Pinocchio," with the&nbsp;
world. In the 140 years since it received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.440
its first literary release, the story&nbsp;
of the living puppet has been adapted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:27.360
almost too many times to count, with&nbsp;
Disney's 1940 animated classic being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.740
perhaps the most well known. So&nbsp;
why was 2022 the year of Pinocchio?

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:36.540
First came the simply titled "Pinocchio"&nbsp;
in September, Disney's live-action remake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.500
of their own animated classic. Helmed&nbsp;
by acclaimed filmmaker Robert Zemeckis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.940
who's known for "Back to the Future" and&nbsp;
"Forrest Gump," it stars Tom Hanks as Geppetto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:50.280
Joseph Gordon-Levitt as Jiminy Cricket, and&nbsp;
Benjamin Evan Ainsworth as Pinocchio himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.600
Unfortunately, this outing didn't quite&nbsp;
reach the lofty status of the original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:00.240
falling flat on its face with critics and&nbsp;
audiences alike despite some impressive visuals.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.920
Not long after, Mexican filmmaker Guillermo&nbsp;
del Toro released his own spin on the tale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.160
with the help of Netflix — and this&nbsp;
one arrived to a much warmer reception&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:12.660
and an Academy Award for Best Animated&nbsp;
Feature. Putting his signature dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:16.800
fantasy twist on the story, which was&nbsp;
brought to life entirely in stop-motion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.660
Del Toro lends an undeniable charm to the&nbsp;
adaptation. "Guillermo del Toro's Pinocchio"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:26.580
features the talents of Ewan McGregor, Tilda&nbsp;
Swinton, Cate Blanchett, and Finn Wolfhard.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:30.180
How many more adaptations of Pinocchio&nbsp;
will make their way to streaming services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.825
over the next few years? We'll&nbsp;
just have to see for ourselves.

00:01:33.825 --> 00:01:35.414
"Oh, hi! It's me!"

00:01:35.414 --> 00:01:37.461
(rabbits grumble)

00:01:37.461 --> 00:01:38.998
"I cannot die!"

00:01:39.451 --> 00:01:40.450
"We know!"

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:41.520
"I can't die!"

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:47.820
Westerns have a long tradition of playing with&nbsp;
iconic trademarks — but in 1991, audiences were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:52.740
given two strikingly similar entries to the&nbsp;
genre: "Tombstone" and "Wyatt Earp." Arriving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:58.020
exactly six months apart from one another, both&nbsp;
films focused on the gunfight at the O.K. Corral,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:02.760
which saw the Earp brothers and Doc Holliday&nbsp;
face off against the feared Clanton-McLaury&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:07.860
gang. As is often the case when two related&nbsp;
titles duke it out in theaters, the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:12.360
to reach the masses is generally considered the&nbsp;
winner in the fight for dominance. In this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:16.260
that proved doubly true: "Tombstone"&nbsp;
didn't just win the box office battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.100
but it has since gone on to be remembered&nbsp;
as one of the best Westerns of all time.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:24.420
While both films dedicate significant&nbsp;
screen time to the infamous gunfight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:29.040
the biggest difference between the two is their&nbsp;
approach. As the name suggests, the focus in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:33.480
"Tombstone" is entirely on the events that led to&nbsp;
the bloody day in the town of Tombstone, Arizona,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:38.160
and it's rounded out with an impeccable cast&nbsp;
that includes big names like Kurt Russell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:43.440
Val Kilmer, and Sam Elliott. On the other hand,&nbsp;
"Wyatt Earp" was more focused on depicting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:48.300
broader life of Earp in its entirety. Starring&nbsp;
Kevin Costner in the titular role, this version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:52.260
shows us everything from Earp's adolescence&nbsp;
during the Civil War to his life in the years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:57.850
after the bloodbath in Arizona. It simply wasn't&nbsp;
nearly as big of a hit with critics or audiences.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:04.440
On a fateful fall night in 1959, four members&nbsp;
of the Clutter family were brutally murdered by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:09.720
home invaders Richard Hickock and Perry Smith.&nbsp;
The brutal nature of the crime — as well as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:14.580
ensuing manhunt for the suspects — led famed&nbsp;
author Truman Capote to the town of Holcomb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:19.020
Kansas, where he found inspiration to begin&nbsp;
writing his groundbreaking novel "In Cold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:23.700
Blood." Widely considered to be the author's&nbsp;
best work, it's credited with kickstarting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:28.860
true crime genre, and it remains compelling to&nbsp;
this day. Nearly 40 years after its publication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:33.780
the story of Capote's struggle to write the&nbsp;
book saw two film adaptations made back to back.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.240
Starring Philip Seymour Hoffman in the lead role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:40.920
the fittingly titled "Capote" follows the author&nbsp;
as he researches the grisly case in Kansas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:45.540
with a focus on his time spent with killer&nbsp;
Perry Smith, played by Clifton Collins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:50.520
Jr. A by-the-numbers drama performed to the&nbsp;
highest degree, "Capote" earned itself an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:55.200
impressive five Academy Award nominations&nbsp;
and snagged a Best Actor win for Hoffman.

00:03:55.647 --> 00:03:57.143
"You think that was too much?"

00:03:57.143 --> 00:03:59.594
"A little bit!"
[laughing]

00:03:59.594 --> 00:04:03.300
Despite being incredibly similar by&nbsp;
nature of telling the same story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:08.400
the competing biopic "Infamous" — starring&nbsp;
Toby Jones and Daniel Craig — strays from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:12.360
its competition with a more down-to-earth&nbsp;
tone. Although it's a capable retelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.420
of the author's life, "Infamous"&nbsp;
struggled to escape from under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:18.840
shadow of "Capote," and never quite&nbsp;
achieved the same level of success.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:25.500
In the summer of 1997, NASA's Sojourner rover&nbsp;
landed on the planet Mars, becoming the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:30.240
successful touchdown on the Red Planet in over&nbsp;
20 years. With Mars now on the public's mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:34.080
filmmakers scrambled to capitalize on&nbsp;
the wave of interest, with "Mission to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:38.760
Mars" making its own landing just before&nbsp;
the spring of 2000. Directed by Brian De&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:43.920
Palma and featuring a solid cast including&nbsp;
Don Cheadle, Tim Robbins, and Gary Sinise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:47.580
the film is set during humanity's first&nbsp;
manned journey to the planet Mars.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:51.840
Unfortunately for the crew, the pioneering&nbsp;
venture soon runs into catastrophe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.040
which is exactly the same fate that awaited&nbsp;
the crew in "Red Planet" when it made its own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:59.520
landing in November of the same year.&nbsp;
A much more bleak look at our future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.320
"Red Planet" tells of a near-future Earth on&nbsp;
its last legs due to a prolonged climate crisis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:09.000
leading humanity to seek refuge on Mars.&nbsp;
Val Kilmer stars as the leader of the crew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.720
of the "Mars-1," a ship sent to the&nbsp;
titular celestial body in a desperate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:17.160
attempt to preserve the future of the human&nbsp;
race. Of course, things go terribly wrong.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:19.620
Like both movie missions to our cosmic neighbor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:24.780
the twin films themselves were outright disasters.&nbsp;
In addition to struggling at the ticket counter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:28.784
"Red Planet" and "Mission to Mars"&nbsp;
were met with equally poor reviews.

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:34.560
The oddly similar animated kids movies "Despicable&nbsp;
Me" and "Megamind" got their cinematic releases in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:39.840
2010, with both films subverting the superhero&nbsp;
formula by focusing on diabolical villains as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:43.920
opposed to the good guys. The first of the two&nbsp;
to appear on the silver screen was "Despicable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:49.173
Me," which saw Steve Carrell play Gru: a&nbsp;
comical baddie who adopts three plucky girls.

00:05:49.435 --> 00:05:51.180
"So good night, sleep tight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:57.180
and don't let the bedbugs bite… because&nbsp;
there are literally thousands of them."

00:05:57.418 --> 00:06:01.198
Aided by his horde of bright yellow&nbsp;
— and highly marketable — minions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.198 --> 00:06:04.980
it's not hard to see why "Despicable Me"&nbsp;
was such a hit with fans of all ages.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:08.520
Dreamworks' effort "Megamind," though —&nbsp;
perhaps hoping to distance the film from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:12.720
the competitive spring and summer release&nbsp;
windows — arrived in the comparably quieter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:17.280
fourth quarter of that year. Telling the story of&nbsp;
the titular alien supervillain — voiced by Will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:21.780
Ferrell — whose hubris causes him to switch&nbsp;
from villain to hero, the plucky comedy was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:26.400
received well by most viewers. It earned a&nbsp;
respectable $300 million in ticket sales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.900
but was still roundly defeated at the box&nbsp;
office by "Despicable Me," which has since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:34.800
received multiple spin-offs and sequels.&nbsp;
"Megamind" has struggled to get a sequel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:38.520
off the ground since its release, with only&nbsp;
a follow-up small screen streaming series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:42.660
currently in development — but it's still an&nbsp;
incredibly fun film with a cult following.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:46.440
Long before "Despicable Me"&nbsp;
and "Megamind," another pair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:50.700
of similar computer animated films took a&nbsp;
tussle in the dirt. It was the late '90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:54.060
and Pixar had just burst onto the&nbsp;
scene with their massive hit "Toy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:58.560
Story," the first feature film to be completely&nbsp;
computer animated. In an effort to keep up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:04.560
newly formed rival studio DreamWorks released a&nbsp;
CGI animated film of their own in October of 1998,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:10.860
a family-friendly romp titled "Antz" — spelled&nbsp;
with a Z. Just over a month later, Pixar fired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:15.240
back with their second feature, "A Bug's Life,"&nbsp;
a film that was remarkably similar to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:20.580
competitor's first offering. That made the fall&nbsp;
of '98 a season packed with bug-themed adventures.

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:25.560
These two might be the most similar on this list,&nbsp;
as both films follow an ant tasked with saving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:29.580
their colony from certain doom. What's far more&nbsp;
interesting than the films themselves, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:34.200
is the behind-the-scenes controversy that&nbsp;
cast a shadow over their releases. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:39.360
to a 1998 article in Entertainment Weekly, the&nbsp;
overlap between the two films was no accident.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:44.700
"A Bug's Life" director John Lasseter accused&nbsp;
Jeffrey Katzenberg — DreamWorks co-founder and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:48.840
former Disney chairman — of intentionally&nbsp;
trying to squash the success of "A Bug's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:54.000
Life" with his own studio's "Antz" using inside&nbsp;
information. Highly controversial at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:56.820
it's somewhat surprising that&nbsp;
not all of the twin films on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:00.972
this list had similar accusations of&nbsp;
plagiarism toward their productions.

00:08:01.139 --> 00:08:06.380
"Don't follow orders your whole&nbsp;
life. Think for yourself."

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:12.360
In the late '90s, reality TV was taking off, and&nbsp;
Hollywood was taking notice. In quick succession,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:17.100
a pair of major releases played off the idea&nbsp;
that a person could find themself the star of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:21.540
their very own program — whether they like it&nbsp;
or not. "The Truman Show" was released first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:26.760
and it capped off a defining decade for Jim Carrey&nbsp;
by putting him in the role of typical suburbanite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:31.320
Truman Burbank. Little does he know, however,&nbsp;
that his entire life has been lived under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:36.240
watchful eyes of television audiences around the&nbsp;
globe, and everything around him is an elaborate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:41.760
carefully-constructed false reality. It all comes&nbsp;
crashing down when Truman gets the idea to finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:46.380
leave his hometown, forcing the people in charge&nbsp;
to try and keep him from discovering the truth.

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:51.300
Following in its footsteps was director Ron&nbsp;
Howard's "EDtv" — starring Matthew McConaughey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:56.700
as Edward Pekurny, the around-the-clock star of&nbsp;
his own reality television program. Unlike "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:01.440
Truman Show," there's no mystery to the plot. Ed&nbsp;
is — at least at first — a willing participant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:06.600
to the producer's latest reality TV series, as&nbsp;
every step of his life is recorded for hungry home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:11.580
viewers. But that lack of mystery, combined&nbsp;
with a plot that just felt like a repeat of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:15.960
"The Truman Show," meant that "EDtv" just&nbsp;
couldn't win over the same audiences. Would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:20.400
we still be talking about "EDtv" if it&nbsp;
had been released first? Maybe so — but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:24.434
it's hard to disagree that "The Truman&nbsp;
Show" is definitely the better movie.

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:28.320
It may seem strange — and it is — but 1997 saw two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:32.100
separate and unrelated biopics based&nbsp;
on the life of the 14th Dalai Lama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:36.840
but told from very different perspectives.&nbsp;
They arrived in the latter half of 1997,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:42.060
with one becoming a box office hit, and the other&nbsp;
going on to become an under-appreciated gem.

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:46.980
Arriving in September that year was Jean-Jacques&nbsp;
Annaud's "Seven Years in Tibet," which focuses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:51.720
on the life of Austrian mountaineer and&nbsp;
former SS member Heinrich Harrer, played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:56.340
by Brad Pitt. Imprisoned in India by British&nbsp;
forces at the onset of the Second World War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.340 --> 00:10:01.440
Heinrich eventually escapes, fleeing to&nbsp;
neighboring Tibet and quickly becoming immersed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:05.820
in their way of life. It's there that he meets&nbsp;
the young Dalai Lama, befriending the spiritual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:10.796
child leader as an impending conflict with the&nbsp;
Chinese government begins brewing on the horizon.

00:10:11.099 --> 00:10:12.179
"Do you like movies?"

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:18.900
"You know, I haven't seen a movie in about&nbsp;
eight years, but as I recall, yes, very much."

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:23.400
A few months later, audiences would&nbsp;
return to Tibet with "Kundun." This time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:25.740
the plot was entirely focused on the Dalai Lama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:30.780
encompassing everything from his selection as&nbsp;
the spiritual leader to his eventual exile from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:36.720
Tibet in 1959. Directed by Martin Scorsese and&nbsp;
reviewed more favorably than its predecessor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:42.060
"Kundun" was nominated for four Academy Awards&nbsp;
that year. It remains a seminal film on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:47.133
complicated relations between China and the region&nbsp;
of Tibet — even if it flopped at the box office.

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:51.420
Every year, cinemas are packed with&nbsp;
animal pictures perfect for audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:55.260
looking for some family-friendly fun, and&nbsp;
there's no creature studios would rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:58.680
turn to than man's best friend.&nbsp;
In the spring and summer of 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:03.120
we got two eerily similar releases that&nbsp;
not only starred some lovable pups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:07.440
but even went a step further by having the&nbsp;
dogs themselves narrate the events on screen.

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:12.120
First up was "A Dog's Journey," released in&nbsp;
May of that year. The first feature film by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:17.280
Gail Mancuso — perhaps best known for her work on&nbsp;
"Modern Family" and "30 Rock" — it's a sequel to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:22.380
2017's emotional rollercoaster "A Dog's Purpose."&nbsp;
Picking up where the first entry left off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:27.420
it continues the story of Bailey across his many&nbsp;
canine lives, as he reunites with his owner time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:31.860
and time again. Close behind was "The Art of&nbsp;
Racing in the Rain," which similarly follows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:36.840
the ups and downs of a dog's life with his human&nbsp;
companion. Told in flashback, Kevin Costner voices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:42.480
Enzo, an aging Golden Retriever looking back&nbsp;
on his racecar-driving owner, Denny. So which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:47.160
heartstring-tugging puppy picture ended up winning&nbsp;
out? Both of these fuzzy flicks wound up in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:52.148
doghouse with critics, but "A Dog's Journey"&nbsp;
brought home a bit more loot at the box office.

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:56.340
Films and television are ripe with&nbsp;
adaptations of the Greek myth of Hercules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.340 --> 00:12:01.560
with films as far back as the '50s and a&nbsp;
hit TV series starring Kevin Sorbo in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:07.080
'90s. The tale of the legendary warrior is a&nbsp;
timeless one — and in 2014, we got not one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:11.700
but two more films to add to the long list&nbsp;
of adaptations. Despite starring a pair of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:15.720
well-known names, neither was able to match&nbsp;
the legend's strength when it came to quality.

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:20.040
The first in theaters was "The Legend of&nbsp;
Hercules," with Kellan Lutz starring as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:24.360
the Greek hero as he embarks on an epic&nbsp;
quest for redemption. Universally panned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:29.160
the film couldn't even recoup its modest budget.&nbsp;
In the wake of that flop was the plainly titled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:33.600
"Hercules," which featured Dwayne "The Rock"&nbsp;
Johnson in the title role. Telling the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:38.820
of a latter day Hercules, the film follows the&nbsp;
aging hero as he wanders as an aimless mercenary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:43.380
When the kingdom of Thrace is threatened with&nbsp;
annihilation, Hercules is left with no choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:47.520
but to rise to the occasion, calling upon&nbsp;
his nearly forgotten strength and courage.

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:52.500
While neither film was particularly good,&nbsp;
Johnson's effort fared better at the box office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:55.380
likely aided by his superstar&nbsp;
name at the top of the poster.

00:12:56.160 --> 00:13:02.640
The first half of 1997 inexplicably saw a pair&nbsp;
of volcano-themed films flow through theaters. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:07.260
fact, they were practically released on top of one&nbsp;
another, with "Dante's Peak" arriving in February&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:12.060
and "Volcano" following in April. Though neither&nbsp;
of the two proved to be a true winner over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:16.980
other, both succeeded in providing plenty of the&nbsp;
fiery mayhem you'd hope for from a volcano flick.

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:21.300
In "Dante's Peak," a dormant volcano near&nbsp;
a small Washington town is on the brink of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:26.700
eruption. The catch? No one but Pierce Brosnan's&nbsp;
seasoned scientist Harry Dalton seems convinced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:31.560
of the impending cataclysm, with his doomsday&nbsp;
predictions falling on deaf ears. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:34.920
a budding relationship between Harry&nbsp;
and the town's mayor — played by Linda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:39.240
Hamilton — complicates matters, and gives him&nbsp;
a personal stake in the community's future.

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:43.980
Dialing up the explosive action to 11, it's&nbsp;
not a small town that comes under peril in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:48.720
"Volcano," but the entirety of Los Angeles.&nbsp;
When violent earthquakes disturb a previously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:53.400
unknown volcano beneath the streets of L.A.,&nbsp;
it sends lava spewing throughout the streets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:57.780
causing untold destruction. While neither&nbsp;
film quite managed to become a classic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:02.520
both are fun, exciting adventures that&nbsp;
should definitely satisfy disaster fans.

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:08.100
England's most famous Prime Minister, Winston&nbsp;
Churchill, remains a symbol of British defiance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:13.320
and one of the most revered leaders of World War&nbsp;
II. Proving that sentiment are a pair of biopics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:18.360
"Churchill" and "Darkest Hour," both of which&nbsp;
were oddly released in 2017. Even though both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:23.640
films follow the same historical figure, they're&nbsp;
fairly distinct in style, tone, and setting.

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:27.240
"Churchill" kicks off in the later&nbsp;
period of Churchill's wartime career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:31.380
set amidst the tense days just prior to&nbsp;
the Allied invasion of Normandy. Here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:35.820
Churchill is played by Brian Cox, and the&nbsp;
film paints him in an entirely different light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:40.800
than history has come to remember. Cox portrays&nbsp;
Churchill as a more cautious and vulnerable man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:45.240
with reservations about seeing through the&nbsp;
impending assault. It's an intriguing study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:49.680
of one of history's best-known figures — but&nbsp;
"Churchill" was immediately met with controversy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:54.466
as some historians took issue with the depiction&nbsp;
of the Prime Minister as a hesitant commander.

00:14:54.466 --> 00:14:59.580
"There are lessons we learned in the last&nbsp;
war. I don't want to make the same mistakes!"

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:00.574
"Of course…"

00:15:00.574 --> 00:15:03.058
"And I shall not be stopped&nbsp;
from speaking my mind!"

00:15:03.058 --> 00:15:07.080
Released later but set earlier, "Darkest&nbsp;
Hour" takes place during the transition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:11.820
of power as Winston Churchill takes over for&nbsp;
the outgoing Neville Chamberlain. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:16.620
the looming threat of a German invasion of the&nbsp;
British Isles has left the nation in a crisis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:19.560
and the newly-elected Churchill&nbsp;
must wrestle with the choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:24.360
between total capitulation or continuing the&nbsp;
desperate fight for survival. Ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:29.640
"Darkest Hour" proved both more memorable and a&nbsp;
bigger success than its eponymously titled twin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:33.900
and Gary Oldman ended up winning an&nbsp;
Oscar for his portrayal of the UK leader.

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:39.360
You've almost certainly seen or at least heard&nbsp;
of "Top Gun," the 1986 hit that launched a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:44.040
young Tom Cruise to superstardom as Naval&nbsp;
aviator Pete "Maverick" Mitchell. The Cold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:48.420
War action flick was the top grossing title&nbsp;
of its year, a feat even its surprisingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:52.440
successful sequel couldn't pull off some&nbsp;
three decades later. With all that in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:55.560
it might surprise some to learn that&nbsp;
not only was "Top Gun" competing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.560 --> 00:16:00.120
another tale of airborne aces in '86, but&nbsp;
it wasn't even the first to see release.

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:05.340
That's right — beating "Top Gun" to the theaters&nbsp;
by four months was "Iron Eagle," a similar story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.340 --> 00:16:10.320
of gutsy fighter pilots duking it out in the&nbsp;
skies. It all starts when Doug Masters, Air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:15.480
Force Academy reject and son of a decorated pilot,&nbsp;
hears that his father has been shot down over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:20.400
fictional nation of Bilya. Springing into action,&nbsp;
Doug turns to reservist Colonel and Vietnam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.400 --> 00:16:25.320
veteran Charles "Chappy" Sinclair to procure&nbsp;
a pair of fighter jets and complete a daring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:30.180
and unsanctioned rescue of his father. Pushing&nbsp;
the limits of campy, improbable action to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:35.580
limit — even for the '80s — "Iron Eagle" defied&nbsp;
its abysmal reception with a trio of sequels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:40.140
but none of them came anywhere close to the&nbsp;
billion-dollar mark like "Top Gun: Maverick" did.

